    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1303">

                <pb facs="1396" n="1382" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg1382"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.1216M/7–1444: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Mexicali</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Ketcham</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Calexico, California</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-07-14">July 14,
                        1944</date>.<lb/>[Received July 15—12:49 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>21. Reference despatch 266 of July 8, and subsequent despatches.<anchor n="23"
                        xml:id="d1303-f1"/> Governor Taboada of Northern Territory Baja California
                    stated this morning he has hopes United States Government would arrive at some
                    solution soon to alleviate critical water shortages Mexicali Valley. As last
                    resort, if water to Alamo<anchor n="24" xml:id="d1303-f2"/> not forthcoming
                    National Irrigation Commission<anchor n="25" xml:id="d1303-f3"/> plans to
                    construct auxiliary canal just below border in Mexican territories to connect
                    with Alamo, although this project involves danger of flooding land of both
                    Mexicali and Imperial Valleys. Later this morning about 300 farmers representing
                    all Ejidos and Colonias affected held outdoor mass meeting at office National
                    Irrigation Commission. Meeting was orderly but Engineer Esquivel, Mexicali
                    manager Commission,<anchor n="26" xml:id="d1303-f4"/> stated farmers threaten to
                    take matters in their own hands and with or without sanction of National
                    Irrigation Commission and use [<hi rendition="#italic">using?</hi>] all
                    available trucks and machinery in Valley, will build connecting ditch direct
                    from river to Alamo Canal, a distance of 500 meters on Mexican soil, unless
                    something is done to supply water immediately. Farmers cannot understand reasons
                    why with water available in river Alamo is so poorly <pb facs="1397" n="1383"
                        xml:id="pg1383"/> supplied and politics in United States and government red
                    tape are blamed. Urgently request Department do everything possible to overcome
                    impasse as good neighbor policy this section seriously threatened.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ketcham</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1303-f1" n="23" xml:id="d1303-f1-content">
